Well, just a bunch of the kids
signed up for free plough rides.

448
00:49:30,155 --> 00:49:33,322
- Oh...
- Your little Natalie is next in line.

449
00:49:33,492 --> 00:49:37,620
Great. You must be the guy
the kids call "Snowploughman".

450
00:49:38,873 --> 00:49:44,247
The kids have a lot of different names
for me but you can call me Roger.

451
00:49:46,172 --> 00:49:48,045
Okay, Roger.

452
00:49:48,966 --> 00:49:51,671
Oh, is that Wayne Alworth?
Hi, honey!

453
00:49:51,844 --> 00:49:57,847
- Mrs Brandston!
- Yeah, that's actually Wayne.

454
00:49:58,017 --> 00:50:00,971
He's just finishing his plough ride.

455
00:50:01,145 --> 00:50:02,723
(HORN BLARES )

456
00:50:02,897 --> 00:50:07,855
- The kids really love to toot the horn.
- What's he writing?

457
00:50:10,571 --> 00:50:14,190
Pleh? Pleh...

458
00:50:14,367 --> 00:50:19,907
That's actually a kind of...
Well, it's a technical term.

459
00:50:20,081 --> 00:50:27,128
Just a ploughing term. Ma'am,
there's a lot of kids on this list, so...

460
00:50:27,297 --> 00:50:30,298
- ..if l could find your daughter...
- Sorry. Sure...

461
00:50:34,262 --> 00:50:40,217
Randy, sweetheart! What are you...
l got him a paint set for his birthday...

462
00:50:40,393 --> 00:50:43,679
Honey, you'll freeze to death.
Come back!

463
00:50:46,941 --> 00:50:49,183
(PHONE RlNGS )

464
00:51:04,668 --> 00:51:06,079
Hello?

465
00:51:06,253 --> 00:51:10,831
Snowploughman, there's someone
here who wants to say hi to you.

466
00:51:13,010 --> 00:51:19,463
Trudy! Are you okay, baby? They
haven't hurt you yet, have they, honey?

467
00:51:19,641 --> 00:51:23,093
- Not yet.
- What do you want from me?

468
00:51:23,270 --> 00:51:27,268
We get our friend back
and you give us a second snow day.

469
00:51:28,400 --> 00:51:33,691
- No way.
- Then we keep the bird, you keep him.

470
00:51:41,246 --> 00:51:42,789
What do you want me to do?

471
00:51:49,088 --> 00:51:51,246
Bill?

472
00:51:51,423 --> 00:51:54,709
Check it out. French fry log cabin.

473
00:51:54,885 --> 00:51:58,669
- You're not wasting your day.
- l know it.

474
00:51:58,848 --> 00:52:02,632
- What's going on? Where's Hal?
- Football field.

475
00:52:03,352 --> 00:52:07,682
<i>He's off building
some kind of shrine to Lady Claire.</i>

476
00:52:07,857 --> 00:52:11,771
And l'm the feeb
who's been sent here to fetch her.

477
00:52:16,491 --> 00:52:19,610
And to think
we used to be afraid of this joker.

478
00:52:19,786 --> 00:52:27,164
How easy is this? We give him his bird,
and he gives us an extra snow day.

479
00:52:27,335 --> 00:52:30,087
l say we go for two...no, three!

480
00:52:30,254 --> 00:52:34,204
Three snow days
and l get to drive his truck!

481
00:52:41,307 --> 00:52:46,053
Where's this mook, anyway?
He should be here by now.

482
00:53:03,705 --> 00:53:07,655
Help! Help!

483
00:53:25,102 --> 00:53:29,099
What if l might actually think
this guy is cute?

484
00:53:29,273 --> 00:53:34,350
What if you might be on the verge of
committing total social suicide?

485
00:53:34,528 --> 00:53:37,149
Help me, please, l beg you!

486
00:53:42,077 --> 00:53:47,035
Anyway, let's pretend you like this guy.
What do you know about him?

487
00:53:47,207 --> 00:53:50,790
He's really ticklish, l can tell you that.
What else?

488
00:53:50,961 --> 00:53:56,881
He hates drum machines. And he once
gave mouth to mouth to a baby chick.

489
00:53:57,051 --> 00:54:02,389
lt was Easter. My mum got the bright
idea of bringing chicks for everyone.

490
00:54:02,556 --> 00:54:08,310
lt was kinda cute till they started dying,
which they did after we named them.

491
00:54:08,479 --> 00:54:14,849
So there's Hal on his hands and knees
giving mouth to mouth to my bird!

492
00:54:15,027 --> 00:54:18,859
lt was...it was...

493
00:54:27,748 --> 00:54:31,449
All right, let's make this quick.
Give me the bird.

494
00:54:31,627 --> 00:54:34,201
- Give me the Wayne.
- The bird.

495
00:54:34,380 --> 00:54:38,841
- The Wayne! The Wayne!
- The bird! The bird!

496
00:54:39,010 --> 00:54:43,055
- You. Leaving. Now.
- l'm going.

497
00:54:44,182 --> 00:54:48,725
But, Claire, you've gotta come with me.
Hal's expecting you.

498
00:54:49,270 --> 00:54:52,604
l know you hardly even know the guy.

499
00:54:53,608 --> 00:54:55,268
But...

500
00:54:57,487 --> 00:55:00,986
You know when you're making
a snow angel and...

501
00:55:01,157 --> 00:55:04,491
..you wanna make it perfect
but you can't because...

502
00:55:04,661 --> 00:55:09,537
..there's always that handprint
you make when you climb out?

503
00:55:09,708 --> 00:55:12,993
Well, with Hal there's no handprint.

504
00:55:21,553 --> 00:55:24,554
Claire! What's Chuck gonna think?

505
00:55:25,557 --> 00:55:28,262
Chuck doesn't think.

506
00:55:32,939 --> 00:55:35,561
- The Wayne!
- The bird!

507
00:55:36,818 --> 00:55:39,025
- You first.
- You first!

508
00:55:40,989 --> 00:55:44,572
- l said " you first" first.
- So?

509
00:55:46,370 --> 00:55:50,368
Just give him the bird.
Please, l wanna go home!

510
00:55:50,541 --> 00:55:54,289
Do it so we can get out of here.

511
00:55:57,506 --> 00:56:01,504
Hi, honey, sweetie. Are you okay?

512
00:56:01,677 --> 00:56:04,631
- Hey, where do you think you're going?
- No!

513
00:56:04,805 --> 00:56:07,296
Let him go!

514
00:56:08,517 --> 00:56:12,100
Nobody messes
with Roger Stubblefield.

515
00:56:22,782 --> 00:56:26,447
Great plan, guys, real great.

516
00:56:39,340 --> 00:56:42,258
You should see what he can do
when he's really trying.

517
00:56:53,021 --> 00:56:56,853
You made this for me? Why?

518
00:56:57,025 --> 00:57:00,607
lt's your favourite animal. The whale!

519
00:57:03,573 --> 00:57:07,523
You know? Nature's gentle giant.

520
00:57:07,702 --> 00:57:11,403
But...l like zebras.

521
00:57:14,501 --> 00:57:16,992
Then how come you have
a whale charm on your bracelet?

522
00:57:17,170 --> 00:57:20,206
You have my bracelet?

523
00:57:20,382 --> 00:57:25,589
- Why does he have my bracelet?
- Destiny. Pure destiny.

524
00:57:30,392 --> 00:57:33,013
Oh, no...

525
00:57:38,984 --> 00:57:42,851
Hey, zip, you're in luck.
The House of Pain now delivers.

526
00:57:43,030 --> 00:57:45,318
Chuck! What do you want?

527
00:57:45,490 --> 00:57:48,990
Claire bear!
l've been looking all over for you!

528
00:57:49,161 --> 00:57:51,734
As for you, Mr Wonderful...

529
00:57:51,914 --> 00:57:54,868
Hey, what've you got there?

530
00:57:55,042 --> 00:57:57,200
lt's all sweaty!

531
00:57:58,295 --> 00:58:01,379
l gave this to Claire.
What are you doing with it?

532
00:58:01,548 --> 00:58:05,961
- l can explain.
- What's he doing with your bracelet?

533
00:58:06,136 --> 00:58:08,674
l wouldn't wanna be you
in about two minutes.

534
00:58:08,847 --> 00:58:12,014
We'll get this back on your lovely ankle
without delay.

535
00:58:12,810 --> 00:58:17,685
Remember where l got this for you?
At Aqualand?

536
00:58:17,856 --> 00:58:20,561
They had the glass-bottom boat,
remember?

537
00:58:20,734 --> 00:58:24,186
The guide kept telling us
to gaze at the wonders of the deep.

538
00:58:24,947 --> 00:58:27,438
All l did was look at you.

539
00:58:27,616 --> 00:58:31,566
Why did you buy me a whale?
l've always wanted to know.

540
00:58:31,746 --> 00:58:36,704
Because of "Shampoo" , the killer whale.
You love that whale.

541
00:58:36,876 --> 00:58:40,660
lt's not "Shampoo" , you blowhole!
lt's "Shamu".

542
00:58:40,838 --> 00:58:42,629
Don't even think about it!

543
00:59:07,240 --> 00:59:10,988
Quite a ladies' man, aren't you?
Craig will be hurting you today.

544
00:59:11,160 --> 00:59:13,367
Hi, there!

545
00:59:47,113 --> 00:59:50,483
l'm sorry. l didn't introduce you
to my friend Steve!

546
00:59:50,659 --> 00:59:53,232
Man, l love this part!

547
01:00:25,944 --> 01:00:27,320
Brandston!

548
01:00:30,741 --> 01:00:35,866
Hello, Syracuse! Winter is finally here,
and you know what that means.

549
01:00:36,038 --> 01:00:40,830
lce sculptures! Kids are whacking
and carving and chipping away.

550
01:00:41,001 --> 01:00:45,462
And, of course, a big idiot
dressed as a magic elf.

551
01:00:45,631 --> 01:00:47,006
Tom, don't do this.

552
01:00:47,174 --> 01:00:51,504
<i>Old Jack Frost here takes you on a tour
of this year's Winter Jamboree.</i>

553
01:00:51,679 --> 01:00:57,931
Hey, that's some car! l'd love to have
that thing in my garage, wouldn't you?

554
01:00:58,102 --> 01:01:03,476
lsn't it amazing what some kids will do
just to earn a merit badge?

555
01:01:03,649 --> 01:01:06,734
- Oh, give me a break.
- lt's Chad Symmonz.

556
01:01:06,903 --> 01:01:11,232
Whenever there's weather
that affects our area, he's there first.

557
01:01:11,407 --> 01:01:15,025
Kids. As unpredictable as the weather.

558
01:01:15,203 --> 01:01:19,035
May l borrow that chisel?
Oh, come on. Want the mike?

559
01:01:19,207 --> 01:01:24,450
Take the mike. lt's for your own good!
l'm telling you! l just wanna...

560
01:01:26,631 --> 01:01:28,256
- Come on.
- No!

561
01:01:38,560 --> 01:01:40,469
Watch it, watch it!

562
01:01:49,112 --> 01:01:50,571
Oh, honey...

563
01:01:55,118 --> 01:01:59,448
No... No. No!

564
01:01:59,956 --> 01:02:02,661
- Chad! No...
- Oh, boy.

565
01:02:04,878 --> 01:02:10,798
Well, the beaver's nice. That about
wraps it up here, Phyllis. Back to you.

566
01:02:15,138 --> 01:02:18,721
<i>- Laura! l've got it!
- Yes!</i>

567
01:02:18,892 --> 01:02:21,679
Patch me in when
everyone in Beijing is ready, okay?

568
01:02:21,854 --> 01:02:25,898
<i>l'm so good. You're on in two minutes.
See ya, bye.</i>

569
01:02:27,734 --> 01:02:33,488
Okay, here we go.
Take the project file, open...

570
01:02:33,657 --> 01:02:35,946
Power pearls, on.

571
01:02:36,952 --> 01:02:42,077
Randy, outside. Randy? Randy!

572
01:02:43,751 --> 01:02:48,543
l want you to come in here right now!
Come in here!

573
01:02:49,882 --> 01:02:55,221
Randy, where are you, honey?
Don't hide from Mummy.

574
01:02:55,387 --> 01:03:00,927
This isn't funny. Mummy
will let you paint anything you want.

575
01:03:01,477 --> 01:03:04,431
You can paint Mummy blue!
Please, don't do this.

576
01:03:06,357 --> 01:03:08,184
Yeah, Nona. Hi, it's me.

577
01:03:10,778 --> 01:03:15,072
Forget the video patch.
Patch me through on the cellphone.

578
01:03:15,241 --> 01:03:17,910
All right, one minute, okay.

579
01:03:19,996 --> 01:03:23,696
No, no, Randy.
Stay right where you are.

580
01:03:23,875 --> 01:03:28,252
- Come on, Mum. Snowball fight!
- This isn't happening...

581
01:03:28,421 --> 01:03:32,086
- Fire!
- Randall Todd Brandston, stop that.

582
01:03:32,258 --> 01:03:36,173
Enough is enough, you understand?
Did you hear me?

583
01:03:38,056 --> 01:03:42,303
Do you want me to tell Mrs Huffner?
Do you? ln the house, right now.

584
01:03:42,977 --> 01:03:46,429
- Yeah...
- Randall!

585
01:03:47,524 --> 01:03:52,814
You little... All right, okay. Wanna fight?
Mummy will give you a fight.

586
01:03:54,322 --> 01:03:58,320
Stop it! You've gotten better
than you were last year.

587
01:03:58,493 --> 01:04:00,320
Hey, what's that?

588
01:04:09,546 --> 01:04:12,167
You... l'm gonna get you.

589
01:04:16,470 --> 01:04:18,877
(CELLPHONE RlNGS )

590
01:04:31,360 --> 01:04:34,479
Sorry, Meltar, l blew it.

591
01:04:34,655 --> 01:04:39,566
l thought l could do it without Hal.
l guess l was wrong.

592
01:04:39,743 --> 01:04:44,619
<i>Where in the name
of the seventh sun are you going?</i>

593
01:04:44,790 --> 01:04:49,499
To the kitchen. l'm gonna make
a grilled cheese sandwich.

594
01:04:51,338 --> 01:04:53,710
<i>Weren't you going to
save the universe?</i>

595
01:04:53,882 --> 01:04:57,797
What do you want me to say?
l lost, evil won, the end.

596
01:04:57,970 --> 01:05:00,426
<i>''l lost, evil won, boo-hoo-hoo. ''</i>

597
01:05:00,598 --> 01:05:05,592
<i>You think l ate a sandwich the day
on Andromeda when l got ambushed?</i>

598
01:05:05,770 --> 01:05:08,142
<i>- l guess not.
- Do you know why?</i>

599
01:05:08,314 --> 01:05:13,391
<i>- Because you're lactose intolerant?
- Quiet! Can't you see l'm talking?</i>

600
01:05:13,569 --> 01:05:19,405
<i>There on Andromeda, things
looked bleak. l, too, had lost a partner.</i>

601
01:05:19,575 --> 01:05:25,495
<i>Fangor had abandoned me and there
l was, up poop river without a paddle.</i>

602
01:05:25,665 --> 01:05:27,704
<i>- And?
- Stranded there alone,</i>

603
01:05:27,875 --> 01:05:32,917
<i>with the fate of the universe hanging
in the balance, l remembered a saying.</i>

604
01:05:33,089 --> 01:05:37,087
Yeah, yeah. "Winners never quit,
and quitters never win."

605
01:05:37,260 --> 01:05:40,011
<i>What? No!</i>

606
01:05:40,179 --> 01:05:43,299
<i>Quitters will be cast
into the flaming pit of death!</i>

607
01:05:43,474 --> 01:05:45,633
<i>Uh, that's gotta sting.</i>

608
01:05:45,810 --> 01:05:48,562
<i>lt's up to you, Natalie.
Gather your forces.</i>

609
01:05:48,730 --> 01:05:52,265
<i>Only you can unleash
the rage of a hundred snow days</i>

610
01:05:52,442 --> 01:05:57,946
<i>that could've been,
that should've been...if not for him.</i>

611
01:05:59,032 --> 01:06:02,531
Snowploughman.

612
01:06:19,803 --> 01:06:24,429
- Oh, man. l thought you were Chuck.
- lt's me.

613
01:06:25,600 --> 01:06:27,308
Yeah.

614
01:06:30,397 --> 01:06:35,438
- So, you escaped the House of Pain.
- Oh, yeah, it was no big deal.

615
01:06:36,778 --> 01:06:40,990
You should've seen me.
lt was unbelievable.

616
01:06:45,621 --> 01:06:50,959
- Tough break back there with Claire.
- Nothing l can't handle.

617
01:06:51,126 --> 01:06:54,626
Come on, Hal! Let me bring you back.

618
01:06:55,297 --> 01:06:59,046
- "But l like zebras."
- A minor setback.

619
01:06:59,218 --> 01:07:04,129
- What is your problem?
- l found that bracelet for a reason.

620
01:07:04,306 --> 01:07:08,055
l don't wanna hear another word
about that crappy bracelet.

621
01:07:08,227 --> 01:07:10,932
lt's not crappy.

622
01:07:13,941 --> 01:07:15,684
What?

623
01:07:15,860 --> 01:07:19,026
- Forget it. lt doesn't matter.
- Yes, it does.

624
01:07:20,197 --> 01:07:22,605
Just leave me alone.

625
01:07:25,578 --> 01:07:28,116
l can't believe l wasted
a whole snow day on this.

626
01:07:28,289 --> 01:07:32,239
What mind-blowing thing
didn't you get to do today?

627
01:07:32,418 --> 01:07:35,917
- You don't wanna know.
- Yes, Lane, l do.

628
01:07:36,088 --> 01:07:37,631
This.

629
01:07:48,810 --> 01:07:52,392
You said anything can happen.

630
01:07:54,983 --> 01:07:57,308
Surprise.

631
01:08:21,926 --> 01:08:26,173
Thank you, Sally and Ken.
We're here on West Burlington Street,

632
01:08:26,348 --> 01:08:30,013
or should l say,
West Buuuurlington Street,

633
01:08:30,185 --> 01:08:36,140
where this record-breaking snowfall
hasn't kept my fans from coming on out.

634
01:08:36,316 --> 01:08:41,025
- Let's move. He was here first.
- We're not going anywhere.

635
01:08:41,196 --> 01:08:44,530
- What do you think of all this snow?
- lt's great!

636
01:08:44,700 --> 01:08:51,616
Kinda caught you by surprise, didn't it?
Caught a lot of people by surprise,

637
01:08:51,790 --> 01:08:57,793
but ol' Chad was in the right place
at the right time, once again!

638
01:08:57,963 --> 01:09:02,921
l'd expect nothing else from
the area's number one meteorologist.

639
01:09:03,093 --> 01:09:05,763
Well, thank you. Hey...

640
01:09:05,929 --> 01:09:11,434
Channel Six's Tom Brandston,
or should l say, Old Man Weather?

641
01:09:14,188 --> 01:09:15,563
Tom...

642
01:09:15,731 --> 01:09:20,393
That storm really fooled me last night.
How did you call it? l'd like to know.

643
01:09:20,569 --> 01:09:23,736
- Just call it a hunch, Tom.
- A hunch?

644
01:09:23,906 --> 01:09:29,245
- Yeah. The cold front...
- Can't learn it in a weatherman school.

645
01:09:29,411 --> 01:09:31,570
l'm just kidding with you there.

646
01:09:31,747 --> 01:09:36,824
l'm sure you learned that the upper
atmospheric shift that we had last night

647
01:09:37,003 --> 01:09:39,707
led to the sudden two point drop
in the Fleeber lndex.

648
01:09:39,880 --> 01:09:45,123
- Of course, Tom. Anyhow...
- There is no Fleeber lndex, Chad.

649
01:09:47,054 --> 01:09:52,594
- You're a fraud.
- Well, you're a joke, Tom. A joke.

650
01:09:52,768 --> 01:09:55,935
l'll tell you what.
To prove you're not a fraud,

651
01:09:56,105 --> 01:10:02,855
why don't you tell everybody here...
Tell us where this comes from.

652
01:10:03,655 --> 01:10:06,192
Yeah, Chad. Tell us.

653
01:10:06,366 --> 01:10:09,651
- What, snow?
- Snow.

654
01:10:10,912 --> 01:10:14,495
Snow. Come on. Well...

655
01:10:14,666 --> 01:10:17,157
You know, the...

656
01:10:17,335 --> 01:10:23,124
The Eskimos have exactly
eleven words for snow.

657
01:10:23,299 --> 01:10:27,297
We, on the other hand, we only
have one word and that word is...

658
01:10:28,304 --> 01:10:29,680
..snow.

659
01:10:29,848 --> 01:10:32,849
When the barometric pressure

660
01:10:33,018 --> 01:10:37,763
reaches all that cold, cold wind
that comes from ol' Canada,

661
01:10:37,939 --> 01:10:44,558
it goes through a process that Tom
knows well is classified information,

662
01:10:44,738 --> 01:10:48,866
and it forms what
we meteorologists like to call...

663
01:10:49,034 --> 01:10:51,489
..the white stuff.

664
01:10:52,037 --> 01:10:55,121
- You're a loser!
- Yeah!

665
01:10:56,500 --> 01:11:01,411
- Could you get in your van and go?
- Sure, l'll go.

666
01:11:01,588 --> 01:11:04,673
As soon as you tell everybody
who really was there

667
01:11:04,841 --> 01:11:08,460
when the blizzard of the century
came knocking at our door, Chad.

668
01:11:08,637 --> 01:11:11,887
- Who was it, Chad?
- Yeah, Chad?

669
01:11:12,057 --> 01:11:15,971
- Tom Brandston.
- Sorry, l couldn't hear that one.

670
01:11:16,145 --> 01:11:18,018
Tom Brandston.

671
01:11:19,606 --> 01:11:23,106
